{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgAQjYM8oH1Sd5BSUrjSu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed `Hijack()` in `response_writer.go`. It used to refuse as soon as `Written()` was true, which includes the point where only headers have gone out (`size == 0`). Websocket libraries such as `coder/websocket` flush headers before hijacking, so they broke. Now it only refuses once body bytes have been written (`size > 0`), and hijacking still works before anything is written.\n\n- I changed the error message to \"gin: response body already written\", since that is now the only case that triggers it.\n- I added a test case to `TestResponseWriterHijackAfterWrite` for \"hijack after header write should succeed\". `go test ./...` passes.\n\nHijacking is now allowed after headers have been sent, but nothing tells the caller those headers are already on the connection. The changes are not committed (you're on a detached HEAD)."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":177,"cache_read_input_tokens":34352,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":177,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7add8747-189f-405b-84fa-5cab9e6ccebd","uuid":"4ca523bd-6bf0-430b-b300-69d315b57a6d","timestamp":"2026-10-18T21:26:32.251Z","request_id":"req_011CgAQjY6zPYBnWHeBf5sUv"}
